WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:02.340
A message disappears.

2
00:00:02.340 --> 00:00:05.030
A community loses its meeting place.

3
00:00:05.030 --> 00:00:09.520
Not because people stopped speaking, but because
someone ordered the

4
00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:12.200
platform to stop carrying their words.

5
00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:16.240
When communication depends on a company’s
permission, that company

6
00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:18.480
becomes a point of control.

7
00:00:19.640 --> 00:00:23.490
PRP opens another possibility: applications
where people communicate

8
00:00:23.490 --> 00:00:26.860
without a platform standing between their
relationships.

9
00:00:26.860 --> 00:00:31.670
The infrastructure carries their exchanges, but
no single platform has

10
00:00:31.670 --> 00:00:33.590
to approve every connection.

11
00:00:33.590 --> 00:00:37.920
The difference is not just who runs the service.

12
00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:40.330
It is where control lives.

13
00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:47.100
An observer may watch the infrastructure without
being able to read protected messages.

14
00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:52.250
Optional cover traffic makes the flow they see
less directly tied to

15
00:00:52.250 --> 00:00:53.970
what people actually send.

16
00:00:53.970 --> 00:00:58.270
Privacy includes the content of a
conversation—and reducing what its

17
00:00:58.270 --> 00:00:59.980
visible activity can reveal.

18
00:01:01.140 --> 00:01:05.560
An address block can silence more than its
intended target.

19
00:01:05.560 --> 00:01:09.100
Unrelated services may disappear behind the same
barrier.

20
00:01:09.100 --> 00:01:13.960
PRP identities do not require allocation by a
central numbering authority.

21
00:01:13.960 --> 00:01:19.270
Blocking a fixed list of identities is not the
same as controlling

22
00:01:19.270 --> 00:01:21.480
everyone’s ability to create one.

23
00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:24.930
Now imagine a peaceful demonstration.

24
00:01:24.930 --> 00:01:28.600
Access through the mobile network is cut off.

25
00:01:28.600 --> 00:01:30.430
The phones still work.

26
00:01:30.430 --> 00:01:34.100
With devices designed for direct local
communication, nearby

27
00:01:34.100 --> 00:01:36.390
participants can keep exchanging messages.

28
00:01:36.390 --> 00:01:41.430
Losing the operator’s connection does not have
to mean losing contact

29
00:01:41.430 --> 00:01:44.180
with the person across the square.

30
00:01:45.350 --> 00:01:49.400
Participating devices can carry messages through
their neighbors,

31
00:01:49.400 --> 00:01:51.430
forming a local mesh.

32
00:01:51.430 --> 00:01:53.460
No mandatory central platform.

33
00:01:53.460 --> 00:01:56.500
No single operator authorizing every exchange.

34
00:01:56.500 --> 00:02:01.560
Reaching beyond the crowd still requires an
available outward path.

35
00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:07.130
But within the square, people can remain
connected through one another.

36
00:02:08.310 --> 00:02:12.540
No protocol removes physical coercion, protects
a compromised device, or

37
00:02:12.540 --> 00:02:15.930
creates a radio link where none can operate.

38
00:02:15.930 --> 00:02:18.040
The goal is not invulnerability.

39
00:02:18.040 --> 00:02:23.120
It is to reduce the places where a single
intervention can silence

40
00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:27.360
everyone—and give people more control over how
their relationships continue.

41
00:02:28.520 --> 00:02:31.240
Freedom needs more than permission.

42
00:02:31.240 --> 00:02:32.870
It needs architecture.

43
00:02:32.870 --> 00:02:33.410
PRP.

